# Commands this build offers that no document a reader is meant to follow
# names. This is a DEBT LIST, not an allow list, and the difference is
# enforced: `tests/check-doc-verbs.sh --reverse` fails on an entry here that
# HAS since been documented, so the file can only shrink. `#` starts a
# comment.
#
# Why a list at all, rather than a green build. The reverse pass was written
# for one defect -- `apex remote pair|devices|revoke|status|enable` shipped and
# appeared in no document, and the forward pass structurally cannot see that,
# because a verb nobody wrote about produces no line to check. Turning the
# direction on found that `remote` was not special: measured on 2026-09-12, of
# the 259 `apex <verb>` and `apex <verb> <sub>` commands in the binary, 127
# were named somewhere and 132 were not. Documenting 132 commands is not one
# change, and a checker that only lands once the whole tree is clean is a
# checker that never lands.
#
# So the gap is written down instead of being invisible. The value is in what
# happens NEXT: a verb added from here on is either documented or shows up as
# a line added to this file, in the diff of the pull request that adds it,
# where somebody can argue about it. Nothing below is endorsed. Every line is
# a thing APEX-OS can do that its documentation does not mention.
#
# Removing a line is the whole point: write the doc, delete the line, and the
# ratchet checks you did both.
#
# 2026-09-12: 132 entries.
# 2026-09-12: 117. `apex host` (8 of 8) and `apex ai` (7 of 7) left, written
#             up as docs/hosts.md and docs/ai.md -- the two worst whole-verb
#             gaps the first measurement found. Still here, and worth naming
#             because they are the next worst: `apex shell` (14), `apex env`
#             (11), `apex task` (7), and section 20's handoff verbs `apex
#             build`, `apex send` and `apex open`, which docs/hosts.md wants
#             to link to and cannot.
# 2026-09-12: 121. Four verbs landed on the integration tip the same day the
#             count above was taken, each documented nowhere and declared
#             nowhere, so the reverse pass -- which is a CI gate -- failed on
#             the tip with four BADs:
#
#                 apex task audit          2814a56d, section 13.14
#                 apex cloudflare preview  7f4475dd, section 13.13
#                 apex secret approve      a67eb704, section 13.8
#                 apex secret approvals    a67eb704, section 13.8
#
#             Declared rather than written up, which is the choice this file
#             exists to offer. Each belongs to a cluster that is undocumented
#             as a whole -- `apex task` is 7 more lines below, `apex cloudflare`
#             3 -- and a single sub-verb written up alone would take its parent
#             off this list on a passing mention, which is precisely the rot
#             the ratchet is here to stop. The number goes UP because these
#             four did not exist when 132 was measured, not because the
#             ratchet slipped: the 132 the docs owed is 117 now.

